Once upon a time I was playing a commercially-produced game. I kept
failing to meet the time limit for performing the necessary tasks to
win (I hate games with time limits). Somewhere on the web I found the
cheat codes for the various powerups and whatnot and used them to
check out the rest of the game which I had never been able to get to.
One of them was a "win now" button. Every now and then I'd hit the
"win now" by accident. I *hated* it when that happened. I wanted to
gawp at the beasties and poke at the landscape and generally hang out,
not see the stupid boring ending cutscene again.

Another time I was playing a different commercially-produced game. I
kept failing to meet the time limit for solving a specific puzzle
necessary to move to the next area of the game (I hate games with time
limits). Somewhere on the web I found the cheat code for bypassing
that little puzzle. I used it every time I played the game from then
on.

If I hadn't been able to bypass that puzzle I would not have continued
playing that game; it was just too irritating. You could say that I
have debased the designer's vision by failing to engage with an
integral part of the game design. Or you could say that by programming
in a "bypass puzzle" button, the designers have given those of us who
can't deal with timed-puzzle-solving an option that allows us to enjoy
their game (and spend money on it).

The point here: pudding farming is not a "win now" button. It's a
"bypass puzzle" button. You can sneer at me for not being able to
survive / not being willing to tackle the end game without three wands
of wishing all you want. You can class it as a second-rate ascension,
one step above explore mode. If I can only survive that way, or if I
just really like playing that way, why do you, who does not farm, is
not forced to farm, can win just fine without farming, care so much as
to remove it?

